    "faq_shrimp": {
      "type": "faq",
      "blocks": {
        "q_dry": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Why does the potato have to be squeezed dry?",
            "text": "<p>Because water steams. Grated potato holds a surprising amount of it, and any moisture left in the pile has to boil off before the strands can brown &mdash; by which point they have fused into a soft cake. Pile the gratings into a clean towel, gather it, and wring it hard over the sink. You should be able to get a startling amount out. That one step is the difference between crisp strands and a pancake.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_method": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Air fryer or oven &mdash; which is better?",
            "text": "<p>The air fryer crisps the r&ouml;sti harder and faster, and it is the better tool if you only care about one thing. The oven is easier if you are cooking both components at once, because everything runs at 425&deg;F instead of two temperatures. If you have one air fryer, do the potatoes first at 390&deg;F and the salmon after at 400&deg;F &mdash; the r&ouml;sti holds its crunch for a few minutes and the salmon needs a rest anyway.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_sauce": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Can I make the sauce ahead?",
            "text": "<p>Yes, up to three days. Make the full batch, keep it covered in the fridge, and divide it into two bowls when you are ready to cook. Do not divide it after it has touched the raw fish &mdash; that is the one thing that cannot be walked back, because the reserved half is served without cooking.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_done": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How do I know when the salmon is done?",
            "text": "<p>By the flake, not the colour. The coating hides the usual visual cue, so press the thickest part with the back of a fork: it is ready when the muscle separates into broad flakes under gentle pressure. Air fryer 400&deg;F for 7&ndash;10 minutes, oven 425&deg;F for 8&ndash;12, and thickness matters more than either figure. Rest it two or three minutes before flaking.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_lingon": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What if I can&rsquo;t find lingonberry preserves?",
            "text": "<p>Cranberry sauce is the closest common substitute and redcurrant jelly works too. Both are sweeter than lingonberry, which is quite tart, so start with three teaspoons rather than four and taste before you add more. IKEA sells lingonberry preserves if you have one nearby, and most Scandinavian and specialty grocers carry it.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_thaw": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How do I thaw the salmon?",
            "text": "<p>Overnight in the refrigerator in the sealed pack, about 12 hours, then pat it completely dry before the sauce goes on. In a hurry, submerge the sealed pack in cold water and change the water every 20 minutes. Never thaw fish on the counter or out of its pack &mdash; it takes on water, and a wet surface stops the glaze sticking.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "q_dry",
        "q_method",
        "q_sauce",
        "q_done",
        "q_lingon",
        "q_thaw"
      ],
